<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anti-Rightist Campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anti-Rightist Campaign was a political purge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> launched in 1957, after the Hundred Flowers movement had invited intellectuals to criticise the Communist Party. More than half a million people, many of them intellectuals and officials, were labelled 'rightists', losing their jobs or being sent to labour camps. The campaign silenced criticism just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>.</p></div>
